1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,840
Buckle up and hold your breath.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:06,640
You just detoured into a world of peril.

3
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:11,240
Danger waits around every hairpin turn high in the Andes.

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:12,940
There is no room for mistakes.

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,540
It is completely unforgiving.

6
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:21,560
And on the deadliest six-mile ribbon of asphalt on Earth,

7
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:24,660
beware the fog and avalanches.

8
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:26,800
There's really no second chances up here.

9
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:28,600
But if it's thrills you're after,

10
00:00:28,601 --> 00:00:30,400
punch that accelerator.

11
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,640
Trust me, there's a ton of adrenaline

12
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,140
going through your body.

13
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:35,940
It's too late for a U-turn.

14
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:39,940
Now you're on dangerous roads on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:58,580
A grim reality exists every time you look at a

16
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,900
behind the wheel.

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Any road can suddenly become deadly.

18
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:10,240
The next drive you take could be your last, whether it's road

19
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:16,360
design, the elements, or other drivers.

20
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:20,960
All it takes is one false move.

21
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:23,860
The most dangerous road of all, according to many, lies in

22
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,500
Bolivia.

23
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,260
Part of a highway system linking Bolivia's capital, La

24
00:01:28,261 --> 00:01:30,260
La Paz and Corrico.

25
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:35,660
It snakes 35 miles through the towering Andes Mountains.

26
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:39,400
Its official name is the North Yungas Road.

27
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,600
But those who dare to drive it, as well as those too fearful

28
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:47,520
to try, call it El Camino de la Muerte, the death road.

29
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,760
Along this narrow strip of dirt perched over vertical drops

30
00:01:55,761 --> 00:01:57,260
of nearly 2,000 feet.

31
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:06,200
Between 200 and 300 motorists perish each year.

32
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,220
Wolfgang Ziegler has driven the death road many times

33
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:16,120
since 1987.

34
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:21,560
The road is as unforgiving of any road that I've

35
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:23,060
ever driven on in the world.

36
00:02:23,061 --> 00:02:26,160
There's no guardrails at all on this road.

37
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:31,660
The road itself is probably the width of an SUV.

38
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:36,300
And you've got vehicles trying to pass each other.

39
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,340
And the vast majority of the accidents

40
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:44,180
are collisions where one of the vehicles or both of the vehicles

41
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:50,380
literally fall off of the sheer cliff that make up the road.

42
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:53,060
Since it opened in the 1930s,

43
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:56,260
has claimed thousands of lives, and many fatalities

44
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:57,860
beyond that have gone unreported.

45
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:03,100
Many of the road's victims have plunged to their deaths

46
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:07,640
during the winter months, when rushing water,

47
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:13,020
falling rocks, and fog make this dangerous road

48
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:15,720
even more treacherous.

49
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:20,580
In the rainy season, which is November through March,

50
00:03:20,581 --> 00:03:23,420
the road basically turns to slime.

51
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:26,920
There's a lot of vehicles that slip off the side of the road

52
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:27,820
in the rain.

53
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:37,560
The best bet to survive this treacherous deathtrap

54
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:40,060
is choosing the right vehicle to navigate it.

55
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:42,440
You need a four-wheel drive vehicle

56
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:46,580
and something that has excellent traction.

57
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:50,580
Be ready for quick maneuvers without over-catching.

58
00:03:50,581 --> 00:03:53,220
Overcontrolling with no room for error

59
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:54,880
is going to get you killed.

60
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,480
There is no room for mistakes.

61
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:02,620
It is completely unforgiving of any carelessness or neglect.

62
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,820
In 2006, the Bolivian government

63
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,940
completed this modern, safer road to be an alternative.

64
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,440
But hundreds still die every year along the old route,

65
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:16,680
as some truckers still drive here, including those that

66
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:19,940
deliver goods to towns that have grown up along the road.

67
00:04:19,941 --> 00:04:24,940
A lot of these truck drivers have made their living

68
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:29,920
on this road, and they get paid by how many times they make

69
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:33,880
this 35-mile trip back and forth.

70
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,460
And they now share the peril with thrill-seeking tourists

71
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:41,960
that flock here for the adrenaline rush of the ultimate

72
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:43,460
driving test.

73
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:48,760
It's a world landmark when you travel to Bolivia.

74
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:53,560
It's like the climbing of Everest when you go to Nepal.

75
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:55,560
It's bragging rights of having driven

76
00:04:55,660 --> 00:05:00,500
on the world's most dangerous road.

77
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,880
Thrill-seeking mountain bikers from around the world

78
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,440
covet those bragging rights too.

79
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:08,720
More than 70,000 have taken the dare.

80
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:16,560
In 2008, an SUV struck and killed a cyclist,

81
00:05:16,561 --> 00:05:20,060
then plunged off the road and plummeted 300 feet,

82
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:21,560
killing eight people in the vehicle.

83
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:28,540
But a road doesn't need blind curves or unprotected hairpin

84
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:29,580
turns to be deadly.

85
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,040
Another of the world's most dangerous roads

86
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:39,980
lies 8,000 miles from Bolivia in the war-torn nation of Iraq,

87
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:43,120
the stretch of road from central Baghdad that leads six miles

88
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:46,560
to the country's main airport.

89
00:05:46,561 --> 00:05:51,860
It's a chilling reminder that danger can also lurk on a flat,

90
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:56,300
paved road with no sharp turns or precipitous cliffs.

91
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:58,000
A trip on this short span of roadway

92
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:00,400
can seem like the longest drive of your life.

93
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:05,520
As a critical traffic artery in and out of the country

94
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:08,120
for both soldiers and civilians, it's

95
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:09,620
a prime target for insurgents.

96
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:21,560
Their weapons of choice are so-called IEDs.

97
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:26,560
An IED is an improvised explosive device.

98
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:29,900
That's where the threat takes almost anything

99
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,320
that they have available, such as leftover artillery

100
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,320
munitions, leftover mortar bombs,

101
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:38,220
or even just packages of explosives.

102
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,020
They'll place them at the side of the road,

103
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:42,460
have some kind of initiation device,

104
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:46,560
and they'll launch that at a target when they see fit.

105
00:06:46,561 --> 00:06:51,560
IED attacks on the road to Baghdad airport became so

106
00:06:51,561 --> 00:06:56,560
irregular that the road was nicknamed IED alley.

107
00:06:56,561 --> 00:07:00,560
To help make this dangerous road safer, the US Army has

108
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:03,560
turned to its experts in Warren, Michigan, at the Tank

109
00:07:03,561 --> 00:07:06,560
Automotive Research Development and Engineering Center, or

110
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:07,560
TARDEC.

111
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:16,560
Here, over the past several years, they've been doing

112
00:07:16,561 --> 00:07:19,100
developing new armor for Humvees and other vehicles

113
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:22,060
traversing the road.

114
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:24,760
The first phase was add-on armor.

115
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:28,300
We had to provide armor protection, not only just the

116
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:32,000
metal and ballistic metals on there, but we couldn't completely

117
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,620
close the vehicle up because folks need to operate.

118
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,120
So we had to also develop and integrate ballistic glass as

119
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:41,860
well to protect our war fighters against IEDs,

120
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:44,160
small arms fire, and those things that they had experienced

121
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:45,960
on the battlefield.

122
00:07:45,961 --> 00:07:49,760
Initially, we could just put a piece of steel up here.

123
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:53,300
But as we needed to go to lighter weight, better performing,

124
00:07:53,301 --> 00:07:56,360
we can use something like aluminum.

125
00:07:56,361 --> 00:07:58,660
But as we needed to go to lighter weight,

126
00:07:58,661 --> 00:08:03,800
we'd go to an S2 glass, fiberglass, combined with a Kevlar.

127
00:08:03,801 --> 00:08:06,440
And we actually use, for even higher performance and lighter

128
00:08:06,441 --> 00:08:08,100
weight, ceramics.

129
00:08:08,101 --> 00:08:10,960
You can think of these ceramics as some that probably most of you

130
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:14,780
have in your kitchen a little bit better performing,

131
00:08:14,781 --> 00:08:15,960
but very similar.

132
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:19,960
For reasons of security, TARDEC engineers

133
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:22,340
won't go into more detail about the composition

134
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:24,940
of its innovative armors or the types of projectiles

135
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:26,040
it uses to test them.

136
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:34,020
We have a number of different types of rounds,

137
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:38,020
both bullets and fragments that we shoot,

138
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:41,480
to see how well an armor design performs

139
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:43,720
against those particular projectiles.

140
00:08:43,721 --> 00:08:44,720
projectiles.

141
00:08:44,721 --> 00:08:46,720
We're looking to balance three things.

142
00:08:46,721 --> 00:08:50,500
It's called the three P's, payload, protection and

143
00:08:50,501 --> 00:08:52,000
performance.

144
00:08:52,001 --> 00:08:55,940
So as we add protection to a vehicle system, that added

145
00:08:55,941 --> 00:08:59,600
weight reduces the payload, the carrying capacity of that

146
00:08:59,601 --> 00:09:00,940
vehicle system.

147
00:09:00,941 --> 00:09:04,300
It also negatively impacts the automotive performance

148
00:09:04,301 --> 00:09:05,600
of the vehicle.

149
00:09:05,601 --> 00:09:09,120
So what we do here at TARDEC is the engineering and

150
00:09:09,121 --> 00:09:12,680
analysis to determine how do you trade off those three

151
00:09:12,681 --> 00:09:14,480
things.

152
00:09:14,580 --> 00:09:16,880
But developing new armor for the army's vehicles

153
00:09:16,980 --> 00:09:21,080
is just part of making this dangerous road in Iraq safer.

154
00:09:21,180 --> 00:09:23,420
Another key strategy is to neutralize the threat

155
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:24,220
on the road itself.

156
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,300
The first step, deploy troops in a convoy of vehicles

157
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:33,240
to run a route clearance operation.

158
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:34,740
Their mission?

159
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,980
Find roadside IEDs before they explode.

160
00:09:37,981 --> 00:09:42,980
Soldiers train for this hazardous duty at Port Leonard Wood

161
00:09:43,080 --> 00:09:44,120
in Missouri.

162
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:45,880
This platform that I'm sitting on here

163
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,620
is what we call the RG-31.

164
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:49,960
We use this platform as front and rear security

165
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:51,520
to our route clearance formations.

166
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:53,500
There are some technologies that we've developed

167
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:56,400
to enhance this particular platform formation.

168
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:58,660
One of those technologies is the mine roller.

169
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:00,340
And that sits out in front of the vehicle.

170
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:04,040
And anything that is subsurface or buried, an IED perhaps,

171
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:05,880
or a mine, is a mine.

172
00:10:05,881 --> 00:10:08,220
an IED perhaps, or a mine.

173
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:10,560
If it's going to explode, we want to explode it

174
00:10:10,660 --> 00:10:12,920
with that roller.

175
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:16,260
The RG-31 also sports a powerful camera,

176
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:18,520
allowing soldiers to identify potential threats

177
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:20,700
from a distance.

178
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,300
How far, they won't say.

179
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:28,340
And the route clearance convoy is bolstered by this battle

180
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:30,440
ready bad boy.

181
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:32,180
Behind me, what you're looking at right now

182
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:35,380
is what we call the Buffalo.

183
00:10:35,381 --> 00:10:38,420
Taller than a tank and fitted with large armored glass

184
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,960
windows, the Buffalo offers soldiers inside a wide and

185
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:45,420
strategic range of view.

186
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:49,120
It also features a robotic arm with a pitchfork-like hand

187
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:52,100
capable of extending to remove suspicious objects

188
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:56,640
from a road surface or for digging up IEDs

189
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:59,440
buried beneath the road.

190
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:01,380
There are several types of IEDs.

191
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:02,940
Some are radio controlled.

192
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:04,140
Some are command wired.

193
00:11:04,141 --> 00:11:05,640
They have to be aware of all of that,

194
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:07,840
so that as they come up on something,

195
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:09,880
they can identify it pretty quickly and easily,

196
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:14,580
get it out of there before somebody's injured by it.

197
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:16,880
This team in Iraq discovered an IED

198
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:19,180
buried just off the roadside.

199
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:20,900
If you don't see any wires, they could still

200
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,100
be around in there since it's remote.

201
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,000
The team successfully unearthed it.

202
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:26,900
We got around.

203
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:27,700
There you go.

204
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,200
We got around.

205
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:32,000
Awesome.

206
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:33,700
Soldiers manning the Buffalo can also

207
00:11:33,701 --> 00:11:36,560
also deploy a remote-controlled robot.

208
00:11:36,561 --> 00:11:39,740
I can't see inside a culvert, so I'll unload this one.

209
00:11:39,741 --> 00:11:44,520
I'll run it down into the ditch, look into a culvert.

210
00:11:44,521 --> 00:11:49,080
If I have a suspected item, then I can deal with it.

211
00:11:49,081 --> 00:11:50,580
If there's nothing there, great.

212
00:11:50,581 --> 00:11:52,360
I put the robot back on the chassis,

213
00:11:52,361 --> 00:11:55,280
and we continue our mission.

214
00:11:55,281 --> 00:11:58,460
But clearing every IED from the road to Baghdad airport

215
00:11:58,461 --> 00:12:02,020
and other roads in Iraq is nearly impossible.

216
00:12:02,021 --> 00:12:08,000
And when they go off, the dangers go far beyond the explosions.

217
00:12:08,001 --> 00:12:11,840
IEDs can create a gauntlet of sizable craters that is difficult

218
00:12:11,841 --> 00:12:13,000
and dangerous to navigate.

219
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:19,900
The Army's answer is a concrete mobile mixer, the next best

220
00:12:19,901 --> 00:12:22,480
thing to a concrete plant on wheels.

221
00:12:22,481 --> 00:12:24,400
A typical civilian ready-to-mix truck

222
00:12:24,401 --> 00:12:27,520
has to report to a batch plant to receive its load

223
00:12:27,521 --> 00:12:29,340
to go out and perform its mission.

224
00:12:29,341 --> 00:12:32,680
One of the advantages of the concrete mobile mixer

225
00:12:32,681 --> 00:12:35,580
over a typical ready-mix truck is

226
00:12:35,581 --> 00:12:37,480
that the aggregate and the Portland cement

227
00:12:37,481 --> 00:12:40,820
remain on the vehicle drive.

228
00:12:40,821 --> 00:12:43,360
Equipped with water tanks, the mobile mixer

229
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:45,920
can easily whip up a batch of concrete at the mission site

230
00:12:45,921 --> 00:12:46,960
in a matter of minutes.

231
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:56,760
Then it's up to the soldiers of the Army's 230th concrete team

232
00:12:56,761 --> 00:13:02,260
the daunting task called rapid crater repair.

233
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,100
It's a race against the clock under threat of ambushes

234
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:09,000
and enemy sniper fire.

235
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:11,800
They finish the job by signing the new section of the roadway

236
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:13,720
with their nickname, the Goons.

237
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:21,120
Then it's off to the next crater.

238
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:23,660
Even if you aren't driving in a war zone,

239
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:26,760
danger of another kind may await.

240
00:13:26,761 --> 00:13:30,760
Because when Mother Nature has her way, all bets are on.

241
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:38,940
One variable can turn even the most innocuous stretch of road

242
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:39,940
into a death trap.

243
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:46,440
The weather.

244
00:13:46,540 --> 00:13:50,880
On average, more than 6,400,000 vehicle crashes occur

245
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:53,320
in the United States each year.

246
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:55,760
And one quarter are attributed to weather.

247
00:13:56,760 --> 00:14:01,760
Statistically, the deadliest kind of weather to drive in

248
00:14:01,860 --> 00:14:03,560
is fog.

249
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:06,760
It can create the most dangerous road of all,

250
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:09,500
one with zero visibility.

251
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:12,300
In this satellite photo, that's not snow

252
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:16,220
draping over 400 miles of California's Central Valley.

253
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:21,160
It's an especially thick ground fog called Thule Fog.

254
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:23,520
You can imagine the havoc at some times

255
00:14:23,521 --> 00:14:26,820
You can imagine the havoc it sometimes triggers on two of

256
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:29,820
the state's straightest, busiest routes that pass

257
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:34,800
through the region, Interstate 5 and Highway 99.

258
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:38,540
If you were to drive into a thick bank of Thule fog,

259
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:43,180
you wouldn't be able to see that fence.

260
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:46,880
You probably wouldn't be able to see 10 or 12 feet.

261
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:50,640
