00:29:59,861 --> 00:30:03,580
perspective. Digitally recorded images of

522
00:30:03,581 --> 00:30:06,380
actual roads are visible through the windshield and all door

523
00:30:06,381 --> 00:30:11,320
windows. And strategically placed monitors

524
00:30:11,321 --> 00:30:14,980
act as side view and rear view mirrors, creating a realistic

525
00:30:14,981 --> 00:30:16,180
experience.

526
00:30:16,280 --> 00:30:17,620
And it's interactive.

527
00:30:17,720 --> 00:30:20,380
So when they turn, the scenario turns.

528
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:22,280
When they stop, the scenario stops.

529
00:30:22,380 --> 00:30:25,520
If they speed up, the scenario speeds up.

530
00:30:25,620 --> 00:30:28,220
The simulator's motion base creates the sensation

531
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:31,460
of accelerating, turning, and climbing and descending

532
00:30:31,560 --> 00:30:32,940
hills.

533
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:35,640
The goal is to determine how drivers respond to hazards

534
00:30:35,740 --> 00:30:39,840
on dangerous roads in a safe, controlled environment.

535
00:30:39,940 --> 00:30:42,080
Studying their reactions and habits

536
00:30:42,081 --> 00:30:44,920
allows engineers to assess a road's design flaws

537
00:30:45,020 --> 00:30:46,980
and correct them.

538
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:50,020
But the simulator also allows engineers to analyze roads

539
00:30:50,120 --> 00:30:52,520
yet to be constructed.

540
00:30:52,620 --> 00:30:54,520
Once trouble spots are identified,

541
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:57,520
it's back to the drawing board.

542
00:30:57,620 --> 00:31:02,840
Trouble spots abound in one nation 7,000 miles from America,

543
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:04,800
where more drivers are killed annually

544
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:08,940
than anywhere else on Earth.

545
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:10,940
Fasten your seat belts.

546
00:31:13,080 --> 00:31:16,080
Statistically, the most dangerous roads on Earth

547
00:31:16,180 --> 00:31:20,080
are in third world and developing nations.

548
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:23,620
More than 85% of all road traffic deaths occur there.

549
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:30,140
No country suffered more traffic fatalities in 2007

550
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:33,140
than China.

551
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:37,840
104,000 perished, nearly 2 and 1 half times more

552
00:31:37,940 --> 00:31:42,020
than in the United States, with 38% of the population

553
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:44,820
vehicles on the road.

554
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:46,880
In China, which has approximately 2%

555
00:31:46,980 --> 00:31:48,880
of the world's drivers, they actually

556
00:31:48,980 --> 00:31:53,820
account for 14% to 15% of the world's traffic fatalities.

557
00:31:53,920 --> 00:31:56,820
India's fatality rate ranks second.

558
00:31:56,920 --> 00:32:01,900
In 2003, 81,000 people died on its roads.

559
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:07,500
By 2007, the death toll rose to 98,000, with an estimated 2

560
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:09,940
million seriously injured.

561
00:32:09,941 --> 00:32:13,940
ROADS IN THE DEVELOPING WORLD AND THIRD-WORLD COUNTRIES

562
00:32:14,040 --> 00:32:16,940
HAVE A TENDENCY TO BE MORE HAZARDOUS THAN OUR ROADS HERE,

563
00:32:17,040 --> 00:32:19,940
PRIMARILY BECAUSE WE HAVE BEEN AT IT FOR A WHILE NOW

564
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:23,980
AND HAVE WORKED TO MAKE ROADS SAFE FOR THE VEHICLES THAT ARE ON THEM.

565
00:32:24,080 --> 00:32:30,000
IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES, THIRD-WORLD COUNTRIES, IT'S A NEW CONCEPT.

566
00:32:30,100 --> 00:32:34,940
THE MAIN ISSUES ARE THESE ROADWAYS HAVE ALL DIFFERENT VARIETY OF TRAFFIC

567
00:32:35,040 --> 00:32:35,940
ON THE SAME ROAD.

568
00:32:36,040 --> 00:32:38,940
LIKE YOU HAVE PASSENGER VEHICLES, YOU HAVE TRUCKS, YOU HAVE PEDESTRIANS,

569
00:32:38,941 --> 00:32:41,840
You have pedestrians, you have rickshaws, you have cycles,

570
00:32:41,940 --> 00:32:44,080
which actually reduces the capacity

571
00:32:44,180 --> 00:32:46,180
and adds the safety concern.

572
00:32:49,480 --> 00:32:51,720
Compounding the danger is that hundreds of thousands

573
00:32:51,820 --> 00:32:55,900
of new drivers take to these cluttered roads every year,

574
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:00,100
from a generation with virtually no car culture heritage.

575
00:33:00,200 --> 00:33:02,360
In the US and in Western Europe,

576
00:33:02,460 --> 00:33:05,300
you've had generations that have operated vehicles,

577
00:33:05,301 --> 00:33:08,600
so people have almost a cultural instinct

578
00:33:08,601 --> 00:33:13,340
on what you do when you get behind the wheel of a car.

579
00:33:13,341 --> 00:33:16,180
Not so in China, where millions of new drivers

580
00:33:16,181 --> 00:33:18,720
are turning the nation's growing interstate system

581
00:33:18,721 --> 00:33:20,880
into a crapshoot.

582
00:33:20,881 --> 00:33:24,560
Today, the Chinese driving along their new superhighways

583
00:33:24,561 --> 00:33:27,060
are high-speed neophytes.

584
00:33:27,061 --> 00:33:28,260
They don't know what they're doing.

585
00:33:28,261 --> 00:33:29,220
They're moving about.

586
00:33:29,221 --> 00:33:30,260
They're changing lanes.

587
00:33:30,261 --> 00:33:32,960
They're using their horns when people can't possibly hear them.

588
00:33:32,961 --> 00:33:34,860
It is chaotic.

589
00:33:34,861 --> 00:33:36,820
Traveling on the Chinese interstates today

590
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:38,660
is like being in the Wild West.

591
00:33:38,760 --> 00:33:39,560
Anything goes.

592
00:33:42,560 --> 00:33:44,700
Beyond improving the education and training

593
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:46,600
of its drivers, developing nations

594
00:33:46,700 --> 00:33:49,100
are enlisting the help of traffic specialists,

595
00:33:49,200 --> 00:33:51,400
like those at Arcadis in Atlanta, Georgia.

596
00:33:55,720 --> 00:33:57,980
They design safe, smoothly flowing roads

597
00:33:58,080 --> 00:34:00,780
based on successful systems developed over decades

598
00:34:00,880 --> 00:34:02,760
in the US and Europe.

599
00:34:02,761 --> 00:34:07,300
We regularly develop simulation models, which always become

600
00:34:07,400 --> 00:34:12,400
lifelike in being able to look at the roadway on a computer

601
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:14,400
as we currently have designed it.

602
00:34:14,500 --> 00:34:16,400
We can create three-dimensional models

603
00:34:16,500 --> 00:34:20,120
that we can communicate with our client.

604
00:34:20,220 --> 00:34:22,620
These models allow engineers to dissect the flaws

605
00:34:22,720 --> 00:34:25,620
of existing roads or highways and determine how they can

606
00:34:25,720 --> 00:34:27,820
reconfigure them to improve traffic flow.

607
00:34:27,821 --> 00:34:34,600
A major focus of companies like Arcadis is urban intersections.

608
00:34:36,600 --> 00:34:39,160
Globally, most traffic accidents occur in intersections,

609
00:34:39,720 --> 00:34:43,660
and design flaws of intersections and developing nations multiply the dangers.

610
00:34:45,100 --> 00:34:50,660
Many have no traffic signals, marked traffic lanes, sidewalks or crosswalks,

611
00:34:50,661 --> 00:34:53,300
creating gridlock and chaos.

612
00:34:53,960 --> 00:34:57,800
There is already so much congestion that everybody wants to get to their destination

613
00:34:57,801 --> 00:35:02,000
as soon as possible, so they kind of get frustrated and break the rules.

614
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:05,780
Case in point, this impatient bus driver in China.

615
00:35:06,580 --> 00:35:10,260
The traffic signal is red, and there are several cars stopped in front of him.

616
00:35:10,680 --> 00:35:14,840
So he turns intentionally into oncoming traffic lanes and runs the light.

617
00:35:15,580 --> 00:35:16,940
Only luck prevents a collision.

618
00:35:18,040 --> 00:35:21,880
Can traffic engineers discourage stunts like this with effective intersection design?

619
00:35:23,100 --> 00:35:27,580
As engineers, we try to lay out the road to properly convey to the traveling public

620
00:35:27,581 --> 00:35:30,620
through signing and marking what is approaching them

621
00:35:30,720 --> 00:35:34,320
at the intersection, proper edge pavement delineation,

622
00:35:34,420 --> 00:35:36,180
right turn arrows, left turn arrows

623
00:35:36,280 --> 00:35:38,960
to delineate which lanes are through lanes, which lanes

624
00:35:39,060 --> 00:35:41,600
are left turn lanes, which lanes are right turn lanes.

625
00:35:41,700 --> 00:35:45,260
And then those are sequenced with the signal phases

626
00:35:45,360 --> 00:35:46,440
at the main intersection.

627
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:51,900
Adding well-coordinated signal systems

628
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:54,280
at problematic intersections like this one

629
00:35:54,380 --> 00:35:56,780
not only helps mitigate the danger to drivers,

630
00:35:56,781 --> 00:36:00,620
but also to pedestrians.

631
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:06,220
Deficient intersection design encourages jaywalking.

632
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:08,420
And half of all traffic fatalities in developing

633
00:36:08,520 --> 00:36:12,320
nations involve pedestrians.

634
00:36:12,420 --> 00:36:14,940
In addition to the roadway design for vehicular traffic,

635
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:18,440
we also try to put in elements of pedestrian access,

636
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:20,740
like the sidewalk that I'm standing on now

637
00:36:20,840 --> 00:36:23,880
will provide proper pedestrian crosswalks

638
00:36:23,881 --> 00:36:26,580
and signalize free-flowing movements for pedestrians.

639
00:36:26,680 --> 00:36:28,420
They'll get their own signal phase as well.

640
00:36:28,520 --> 00:36:29,880
So that can safely and efficiently

641
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:32,520
move the pedestrians safely through an intersection

642
00:36:32,620 --> 00:36:33,520
avoiding conflict.

643
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:39,960
Avoiding conflict is the last thing

644
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:42,700
on the minds of these risk-takers

645
00:36:42,800 --> 00:36:45,440
who create their own version of a dangerous road

646
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:47,900
and flip over the prospect of navigating.

647
00:36:47,901 --> 00:36:54,400
The most unusual kind of dangerous road isn't a road at all.

648
00:36:54,500 --> 00:36:55,400
It's off-road.

649
00:37:09,960 --> 00:37:11,860
And a special breed of competitors,

650
00:37:11,960 --> 00:37:14,560
like this bunch outside of Cedar City, Utah,

651
00:37:14,660 --> 00:37:16,560
is pushing the boundaries of off-roading

652
00:37:16,561 --> 00:37:17,800
to new heights.

653
00:37:25,300 --> 00:37:27,900
They relish the challenge of maneuvering highly modified

654
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:32,000
trucks, or buggies, over terrain that most casual off-roaders

655
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:33,820
would consider impossible to navigate.

656
00:37:40,020 --> 00:37:45,360
Their sport, aptly enough, is called rock crawling.

657
00:37:45,361 --> 00:37:48,360
It feels like a roller coaster every single course.

658
00:37:48,361 --> 00:37:50,360
You know, a lot of new people come out and watch,

659
00:37:50,361 --> 00:37:52,360
and they think, man, this is insane.

660
00:37:52,361 --> 00:37:53,360
I can't believe you guys do this.

661
00:37:53,361 --> 00:37:57,360
One thing that all these guys have in common is just going

662
00:37:57,361 --> 00:38:00,360
on a typical trail ride isn't quite enough for them.

663
00:38:00,361 --> 00:38:02,360
They want to be pushed to do something extreme,

664
00:38:02,361 --> 00:38:05,360
not just go out and do the same thing everyone else is.

665
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:11,360
And you start feeling comfortable being at 70-degree angles

666
00:38:11,361 --> 00:38:15,200
up or down and 50-degree angles sideways.

667
00:38:15,300 --> 00:38:17,800
The competition is different because it's usually

668
00:38:17,900 --> 00:38:20,700
a rock pile someplace, and we're just trying

669
00:38:20,800 --> 00:38:23,900
to drive up the most extreme thing that the course designer

670
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:24,900
can think of.

671
00:38:28,820 --> 00:38:30,720
Nicole Johnson, a mother of two,

672
00:38:30,820 --> 00:38:34,620
has been driving rock crawlers competitively since 2001.

673
00:38:34,720 --> 00:38:36,820
I think it's really exciting, actually,

674
00:38:36,920 --> 00:38:38,760
to be behind the wheel of this car.

675
00:38:38,860 --> 00:38:41,360
When you're driving up a big rock, sometimes all you have

676
00:38:41,361 --> 00:38:42,860
is sky and clouds.

677
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:44,500
When you're coming off of something steep,

678
00:38:44,600 --> 00:38:47,160
you just see ground, and you just hope you can run it out

679
00:38:47,260 --> 00:38:49,600
and not roll.

680
00:38:49,700 --> 00:38:51,100
OK, drive forward.

681
00:38:51,200 --> 00:38:52,300
Three wheels.

682
00:38:52,400 --> 00:38:53,900
Nicole, like every driver in rock crawling,

683
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:56,500
depends on a spotter to act as a second set of eyes

684
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:58,520
and direct the overall effort.

685
00:38:58,620 --> 00:38:59,320
Go up over there.

686
00:38:59,420 --> 00:39:00,520
We're backing down.

687
00:39:00,620 --> 00:39:03,320
Nicole's spotter is her husband, Frank.

688
00:39:03,420 --> 00:39:06,820
There's just little cues that you look for in the suspension

689
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:09,820
and the way the car is built that you could kind of tell

690
00:39:09,821 --> 00:39:12,580
that, oh, OK, we got it, or now we got to back off.

691
00:39:12,680 --> 00:39:14,360
Steer me. OK, stop.

692
00:39:14,460 --> 00:39:16,060
The driver might get all of the glory,

693
00:39:16,160 --> 00:39:18,260
but it's the spotter that does all the work.

694
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:19,560
The spotter's the poor guy that's

695
00:39:19,660 --> 00:39:21,660
got to run around the vehicle and pulling on ropes

696
00:39:21,760 --> 00:39:24,160
and stacking rocks and pointing us where we need to go.

697
00:39:27,580 --> 00:39:29,980
The spotters face the greatest dangers in rock crawling.

698
00:39:32,880 --> 00:39:33,580
This one.

699
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:36,780
Straighten your wheels.

700
00:39:36,880 --> 00:39:39,820
Especially in the all too likely event of a rollover.

701
00:39:39,821 --> 00:39:43,060
Spotters falling down cliffs and hurting their legs

702
00:39:43,160 --> 00:39:44,760
or their knees or any part of their body, really,

703
00:39:44,860 --> 00:39:47,060
if the spotter's in a bad position,

704
00:39:47,160 --> 00:39:48,860
they could get run over.

705
00:39:48,960 --> 00:39:50,060
More route!

706
00:39:50,160 --> 00:39:51,060
More route!

707
00:39:51,160 --> 00:39:51,860
Way to go, Mike!

708
00:39:51,960 --> 00:39:53,260
Go, go!

709
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:56,300
For Nicole and Frank, the secret to minimizing

710
00:39:56,400 --> 00:40:00,640
the danger is trust and good communication.

711
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:01,680
Drive forward!

712
00:40:01,780 --> 00:40:03,680
Doesn't feel right.

713
00:40:03,780 --> 00:40:04,780
Drive forward.

714
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:05,780
There you go.

715
00:40:05,880 --> 00:40:06,880
You really have to trust.

716
00:40:06,980 --> 00:40:07,820
Sometimes a car feels really weird,

717
00:40:07,920 --> 00:40:09,820
feels like you're going to roll it down.

718
00:40:09,821 --> 00:40:12,320
when you're really not, and he can tell if it's going to.

719
00:40:12,321 --> 00:40:13,900
Rear dig down. Rear dig down.

720
00:40:13,901 --> 00:40:15,320
Down this way. Yeah.

721
00:40:15,321 --> 00:40:17,820
Your ass hand has to be that way, Nicole.

722
00:40:17,821 --> 00:40:19,160
I'm trying, Frank.

723
00:40:19,161 --> 00:40:22,240
Our way of dealing with it, maybe we yell at each other.

724
00:40:22,241 --> 00:40:24,340
Work left. Go left now. Go left.

725
00:40:24,341 --> 00:40:26,000
All the way left. Working left.

726
00:40:26,001 --> 00:40:27,040
That's right.

727
00:40:27,041 --> 00:40:28,680
Go right. Whatever that is.

728
00:40:28,681 --> 00:40:32,300
But it's all, you know, water under the bridge at that point.

729
00:40:32,301 --> 00:40:34,020
We're here to have fun, and that's what about.

730
00:40:34,021 --> 00:40:37,520
We had a lot of fights today, but, you know, we're smiling.

731
00:40:37,521 --> 00:40:39,520
It's all good.

732
00:40:39,620 --> 00:40:40,960
It's good.

733
00:40:41,060 --> 00:40:43,060
Beyond good teamwork, competitors

734
00:40:43,160 --> 00:40:47,560
counter the sport's inherent dangers with technology.

735
00:40:47,660 --> 00:40:49,460
Your dangers are really high.

736
00:40:49,560 --> 00:40:51,460
If you're not wearing your proper safety harnesses,

737
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:54,000
if you're not wearing your helmet or fire suits

738
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:57,100
or anything like that, the danger is always there.

739
00:40:57,200 --> 00:40:58,940
It's any kind of motor sport.

740
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:05,980
Rock crawling is a very dangerous sport.

741
00:41:05,981 --> 00:41:07,680
we do treat it with respect.

742
00:41:07,780 --> 00:41:09,780
We make sure that we have the best of the best

743
00:41:09,880 --> 00:41:13,220
as far as a suspension seat, the strongest seatbelts

744
00:41:13,320 --> 00:41:15,720
that we can get.

745
00:41:15,820 --> 00:41:17,860
And these modified monsters also

746
00:41:17,960 --> 00:41:22,220
sport specialized tires, available only for competitors,

747
00:41:22,320 --> 00:41:24,440
tailor-made for the task.

748
00:41:24,540 --> 00:41:26,100
These are not your normal street tires.

749
00:41:26,200 --> 00:41:28,140
These are 37-inch Maxxis Trepidors.

750
00:41:28,240 --> 00:41:31,080
And as you can see just by the tread, they're ultra-sticky.

751
00:41:31,180 --> 00:41:33,040
And this is what keeps us clinging to those rocks

752
00:41:33,140 --> 00:41:35,440
on those side hills, on those big climbs.

753
00:41:35,441 --> 00:41:40,440
climbs, this is what gets us up there.

754
00:41:40,441 --> 00:41:42,440
But it's more than just enormous tires that get these

755
00:41:42,441 --> 00:41:47,440
buggies around these rocks. It also takes state-of-the-art

756
00:41:47,441 --> 00:41:54,440
suspension systems. The suspension is known as a

757
00:41:54,441 --> 00:41:56,440
four-lane. As you can see, there are two lower trailing

758
00:41:56,441 --> 00:41:59,440
arms and two upper trailing arms. Those upper trailing

759
00:41:59,441 --> 00:42:01,440
arms are actually what locate the axle underneath the

760
00:42:01,441 --> 00:42:05,440
vehicle, and then the lowers simply provide support.

761
00:42:05,441 --> 00:42:08,180
This particular suspension gives us great movement up

762
00:42:08,280 --> 00:42:10,740
and down for wheel travel and really helps us

763
00:42:10,840 --> 00:42:13,240
cling to those hard side hills and those nasty crawling

764
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:15,380
sections.

765
00:42:15,480 --> 00:42:16,980
And specialized brake controls

766
00:42:17,080 --> 00:42:19,780
allow independent operation of the front and rear brakes.

767
00:42:22,600 --> 00:42:25,200
While most of us do the best we can to avoid danger

768
00:42:25,300 --> 00:42:30,200
when we're behind the wheel, these thrill seekers pursue it

769
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:32,840
and savor its taste.

770
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:34,740
When you're hanging off these cliffs

771
00:42:34,741 --> 00:42:38,640
and you're looking down ready to roll a vehicle,

772
00:42:38,740 --> 00:42:42,740
or you're leaning sideways and having to pull an awesome save,

773
00:42:42,840 --> 00:42:45,560
trust me, there's a ton of adrenaline going through your

774
00:42:45,660 --> 00:42:46,760
body.

775
00:42:46,860 --> 00:42:48,880
I think you have to be a little bit of an adrenaline junkie.

776
00:42:48,980 --> 00:42:53,800
And I can't think of any other sport that I would rather do.

777
00:42:53,900 --> 00:42:57,020
For these extreme sportsmen, the more clear and present

778
00:42:57,120 --> 00:43:00,640
